{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"The bank isn’t identified, so its specific contract controls.\n\n| | Unitary-fee waiver | Ordinary expense limitation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **What is recovered?** | Previously waived advisory fees. The adviser ordinarily remains responsible for expenses covered by the unitary fee. | Previously waived fees and/or expenses paid by the adviser to keep fund expenses below a contractual cap. |\n| **What constrains recovery?** | The waiver’s express recoupment provisions and applicable fee or expense ceilings—not automatically the ordinary expense-cap test. | Typically, recovery cannot push expenses above the **lower of the cap when relief was provided and the cap when reimbursement occurs**, using contractual exclusions. |\n| **Timing** | Contract-specific; recoupment must be expressly authorized. | Commonly a rolling three-year period, but contract-specific. |\n\n**Worth knowing:** A unitary fee describes who bears operating costs; it does not itself establish a right to recover waived fees."}}
